{"name":"Drip Coffee","hex":"#7a280a","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"drip coffee","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Coffee made by brewing hot water through a filter that drips into a carafe or other vessel."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/drip_coffee"}],"entries":[{"title":"Drip coffee","blocks":[{"text":"Drip coffee is made by pouring hot water onto ground coffee beans, allowing it to brew while seeping through. There are several methods for doing this, including using a filter. Terms used for the resulting coffee often reflect the method used, such as drip-brewed coffee, or, somewhat inaccurately, filtered coffee in general. Manually brewed drip coffee is typically referred to as pour-over coffee. Water seeps through the ground coffee, absorbing its constituent chemical compounds, and then passes through a filter. The used coffee grounds are retained in the filter, while the brewed coffee is collected in a vessel such as a carafe or pot."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a8/TEDxAMS2011_044.JPG/330px-TEDxAMS2011_044.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Drip_coffee","wikidata":"Q1155703"},{"title":"Coffee","blocks":[{"text":"Coffee is a beverage brewed from roasted, ground coffee beans. Dark-colored and bitter, coffee has a stimulating effect on humans due to its caffeine content; however decaffeinated coffee is also commercially available. There are also various coffee substitutes."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e4/Latte_and_dark_coffee.jpg/330px-Latte_and_dark_coffee.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Coffee","wikidata":"Q8486"},{"title":"Coffee (color)","blocks":[{"text":"Coffee is a brownish color that is a representation of a roasted coffee bean. Different types of coffee beans have different colors when roasted—the color coffee represents an average."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c5/Roasted_coffee_beans.jpg/330px-Roasted_coffee_beans.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Coffee_(color)","wikidata":"Q2932937"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/62/Drip_Coffee_Bangkok.jpg/500px-Drip_Coffee_Bangkok.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/62/Drip_Coffee_Bangkok.jpg/1280px-Drip_Coffee_Bangkok.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/62/Drip_Coffee_Bangkok.jpg/500px-Drip_Coffee_Bangkok.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Coffee drips through coffee grounds and filters into several jars in a specialty coffee shop.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ADrip_Coffee_Bangkok.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/df/Manual_drip_%28pour-over%29_coffee.jpg/500px-Manual_drip_%28pour-over%29_coffee.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/df/Manual_drip_%28pour-over%29_coffee.jpg/1280px-Manual_drip_%28pour-over%29_coffee.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/df/Manual_drip_%28pour-over%29_coffee.jpg/500px-Manual_drip_%28pour-over%29_coffee.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Manual drip (pour-over) coffee","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AManual_drip_(pour-over)_coffee.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c2/Coffee_Pour_Over_Setup.jpg/500px-Coffee_Pour_Over_Setup.jpg?utm_so